number|title|condition|evidence|remediation|limitation|attack|nist|nist_rationale|sources|severity
001|Effective policy still permits NTLMv1|The assessed authentication endpoint permits NTLMv1 where the selected applicable baseline requires its removal.|Resultant authentication policy; OS/build; effective endpoint configuration; compatibility exception.|Remove NTLMv1 through a tested migration and enforce the selected supported policy.|NTLM use is not necessarily NTLMv1. Inventory and audit evidence are not password-cracking proof.|T1110.002;T1557|IA-2;IA-5;SC-8|Protect authentication against weak protocol and credential-handling modes.||High
002|SMBv1 is enabled without an approved requirement|SMBv1 is effectively available on an assessed server contrary to the selected applicable baseline.|Installed feature and service state; OS/build; effective SMB configuration; approved exception.|Remove unneeded SMBv1 using the supported server-role guidance.|Feature presence does not establish network exposure or a specific exploitable vulnerability.|T1210|CM-7;SI-2|Reduce obsolete service functionality and its potential exploitation surface.|MS-SMB|Medium
003|SMB signing is not required where the baseline requires it|The effective SMB client or server role does not require signing according to the selected baseline profile.|Effective client/server signing configuration; OS/build; policy precedence; selected baseline.|Require supported SMB signing and validate compatibility before rollout.|Client and server settings differ. Encryption, protocol behavior, and connection context affect the resulting protection.|T1557.001|SC-8;CM-6|Protect integrity and authenticity of SMB communication.|MS-SMB|High
004|A DC does not enforce the required LDAP signing policy|Effective DC LDAP signing behavior falls below the selected applicable requirement.|DC role; effective signing policy; OS/build; supported audit evidence; baseline version.|Enforce supported LDAP signing after identifying incompatible clients.|LDAP over TLS and unsigned SASL binds need separate treatment; do not infer behavior from one client setting.|T1557|SC-8;CM-6|Protect the integrity of directory authentication and communication.|MS-LDAP|High
005|LDAP channel binding is below the applicable requirement|A TLS-protected LDAP endpoint accepts authentication without the channel-binding protection required by the selected applicable profile.|Effective CBT policy; supported OS/update state; endpoint authentication mode; compatibility evidence.|Apply supported channel-binding enforcement with staged client remediation.|Channel binding is not synonymous with LDAP signing. Unsupported modes must be NotApplicable with evidence.|T1557|SC-8;IA-2;CM-6|Bind authenticated sessions to their protected transport channel.|MS-LDAP|High
006|Resultant password policy falls below the approved requirement|The effective fine-grained or domain password policy for an in-scope identity fails a defined organization-specific requirement.|Resultant policy selection; account scope; policy values; approved requirement and exception.|Apply the appropriate supported password policy and remove unintended exclusions.|Do not invent a universal rotation age or claim password quality from policy settings alone.|T1110|IA-5;CM-6|Govern authenticators using the applicable account policy.||Medium
007|Managed local administrator password protection is not configured as required|An in-scope server lacks the required effective Windows LAPS configuration or approved equivalent control.|Server role; managed account; LAPS policy; storage mode; protected metadata and approved alternatives.|Configure the approved local administrator credential-management control.|Do not retrieve passwords or assume an absent AD LAPS attribute means Entra-backed LAPS is absent.|T1078.003|IA-5;AC-6|Manage local administrative authenticators and reduce unmanaged reuse.|MS-LAPS|High
008|Seamless SSO key rotation is outside the approved interval|An active Seamless SSO account has rotation metadata older than the documented supported operational requirement.|Active SSO forest binding; account password-last-set metadata; approved interval; change records.|Perform supported SSO key rotation through change control and validate sign-in health.|Age alone is not evidence the key is compromised. An inactive leftover account is evaluated as lifecycle posture.|T1556.007|IA-5;CM-3|Maintain the lifecycle of the authenticator used by the SSO trust.|MDI-HYBRID|Medium
